            'body_en' => "In 1963 a survey team from the universities of Istanbul and Chicago walked over a low mound near Şanlıurfa, noted the flint tools scattered on it and the broken limestone slabs that looked like gravestones, and moved on. For thirty years the hill was left to its wheat and its mulberry tree.\n\nIn October 1994 the German archaeologist Klaus Schmidt, reading the old survey, went to see it for himself. A farmer of the Yıldız family, whose land it was, led him up the slope. Schmidt understood at once that the \"gravestones\" were the tops of monumental pillars, and that the hill itself was not natural: fifteen metres of it had been built up by people. He began digging the next year and spent the rest of his life here.\n\nWhat came out of the ground overturned the textbook order of things. The textbook said that farming came first, then villages, then surplus, then temples. Göbekli Tepe was raised by people who still hunted gazelle and gathered wild grain — and there is no clear sign of cultivation at the site. The monument, it seems, came before the farm.",
